The files in this package are a Kotlin port of a subset of
material-color-utilities, vendored under the Apache License 2.0 (see LICENSE
beside this NOTICE).

Upstream: https://github.com/material-foundation/material-color-utilities
Copyright 2021 Google LLC
Ported: 2026-10-03 (DEC-097, ADR-0015)

Why vendored: the upstream project publishes no first-party Maven artifact on
Maven Central or Google Maven — probed 2026-09-30 (CONF-50) — and the accent
policy of UI_SPEC.md §5.4 needs exactly three of its algorithms:
QuantizerCelebi (a Wu quantizer refined by k-means), Score (the population and
chroma ranking) and TonalPalette (hue/chroma to a tone-30 container). The port
follows those algorithms; deviations are listed here.

Ports and simplifications, one per line:
- The colour maths is NOT upstream's sRGB -> XYZ -> CAM16 -> HCT pipeline: this
  port works in HSL terms, so `chroma` is the channel spread
  ((max - min) / 255 * 100) and `hue` comes from the channel order. Before
  TASK-045 the port also placed `tone` as the **mean channel** of the built
  colour rather than the CIE L* value upstream defines; that made the container
  sit at L* 40-57 instead of 30, so on-surface text over a saturated accent fell
  to 2.8:1 and UI_SPEC.md 5.4's "4.5:1 or better" did not hold. The port now
  solves the tone in L* — the same sRGB transfer function and Y weights the
  contrast record uses — and reproduces the six reference accents UI_SPEC.md
  records (three exactly, three within one 8-bit step). Where a tone of 30 cannot
  hold the requested channel spread inside 0..255, the packed colour clamps and
  the spread is reduced, which is what upstream's gamut search does to chroma.
  The three rules the specification fixes (hue from the scored colour, chroma
  clamped to [24, 48], container at tone 30) are kept, and the derived container
  is measurable and deterministic, which is what TEST-A11Y-002 records; but its
  colours are not bit-identical to what the CAM16 path would produce, and the
  contrast record, not upstream, is the authority for their readability.
- QuantizerCelebi keeps upstream's two-stage shape (histogram pass refined by
  k-means over twelve iterations), with two differences: the starting palette
  is the most populous 5-bit histogram bins rather than upstream's Wu
  variance-minimising pass, and the histogram is 5 bits per channel.
- Score keeps upstream's weights (target chroma 48, proportion 0.7, above 0.3,
  below 0.1, cutoff chroma 5, excited-proportion cutoff 1.4 %), applied to the
  HSL-style chroma above rather than CAM16 chroma.
- The inverse mapping (hue/chroma/tone -> sRGB) is a direct HSL construction
  documented at its call site, not upstream's HctSolver gamut search: the
  requested channel spread is placed around a zero base and the tone is then
  solved as the CIE L* the port's own documentation defines, so a caller can
  assert the returned colour's spread and lightness directly.

No other file in this repository is derived from upstream.
